<div>Am 16.10.2026 um 11:40 schrieb Alex Morgan
&lt;alex.morgan@kestrel-eng.co.uk&gt;:</div>
<blockquote style="margin:0 0 0 .8ex;border-left:1px solid
#ccc;padding-left:1ex">
<p>Dear Julia, dear Felix,</p>
<p>Thank you for the draft Master Supply Agreement. Ahead of our full
comments next week, I attach Kestrel's mark-up of clause 12 (liability),
because it is the point most likely to need discussion. The changes are
tracked and each one has a comment explaining it. In summary:</p>
<p>1. General cap: 100% of the Annual Fees, instead of 200%. This reflects
the value of the supply and market practice for equipment of this kind.<br>
2. Data breach: Kestrel cannot accept unlimited liability. We propose a
separate cap of two times the Annual Fees for data breach and breaches of
clause 10, in addition to the general cap.<br>
3. Indirect loss: we have replaced Kestrel's liability for indirect and
consequential loss, including loss of production, with a mutual exclusion.
Unlimited exposure to production stoppage costs is not insurable for an
equipment supplier.</p>
<p>The carve-outs in clause 12.3 (injury to life, body or health; intent
and gross negligence; product liability; guarantees) stay as they are.</p>
<p>We are happy to discuss this on a call. Tom will suggest some times.</p>
<p>Kind regards<br>
Alex</p>
<p>Alex Morgan<br>
Senior Legal Counsel<br>
Kestrel Engineering Ltd<br>
T +44 114 496 0230<br>
alex.morgan@kestrel-eng.co.uk</p>
<p>This email may contain confidential and legally privileged information.
If you have received it in error, please tell the sender and delete it.</p>
